course: dictionary
lesson: 5548
title: Brand names and everyday words
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Lego is a brand name. A brand name is the special name a company gives its product, and it starts with a capital letter. LEGO is the company, and Lego is the toy. People also use the name like an ordinary noun, as in «a box of Lego».
explain: You can use context to work out what a word means. «Max got a Lego set for his birthday; his brother built the spaceship from it.» A set is a box of pieces sold together, and building a spaceship from it tells you the pieces click together to make things.
explain: When you mean the toy in general, you say «build with Lego», so Lego is uncountable. When you mean the bricks themselves, you can count them: «a Lego brick», «Lego bricks». Choose the form that fits the sentence.
explain: Writers also use Lego to make comparisons. «The code is made of coloured blocks that snap together like Lego bricks» tells you the blocks join up in an easy, secure way, just as Lego bricks do.
fact: Lego is a toy made of small plastic bricks that click together so you can build things.
fact: LEGO is a Danish company founded in 1932; its name comes from the Danish «leg godt», meaning «play well».
fact: Lego can mean the toy in general, one set, or the pieces themselves.
fact: Tiny Lego pieces can choke babies and toddlers, so keep them away from little ones.
fact: Treading on Lego in bare feet really hurts.
exercise: «Max got a Lego set for his birthday.» What is a set? | *A box of pieces sold together | A grown-up's toy car | A kind of birthday cake | A place to sleep
exercise: «Atoms rarely stay alone: they join up into groups called molecules, a bit like Lego bricks clicking together.» What does the comparison tell you? | Atoms are sold in boxes | Atoms are made of plastic | *Atoms join up in groups, like bricks clicking together | Atoms are very large and heavy
exercise: Which word in this list is a brand name? | *LEGO | founded | Danish | company
test: Why should Lego pieces be kept away from toddlers? | Because they are too heavy to lift | Because Lego is always too hot | Because toddlers cannot count bricks | *Because tiny pieces can choke them
test: «Numbers are like Lego: take a ten, click on a unit with a hyphen, and you have a number.» What does like Lego suggest? | Numbers are made of plastic | Numbers are always red | *Numbers are built by joining parts together | Numbers are sold in sets
exam: Why does the word Lego start with a capital letter in «a box of Lego»? | Because it is the start of a sentence | Because it is always in a title | Because it is a Danish word for play | *Because it is the name of a brand
